# Why Immutable?

When you call `stateSubject.next(newValue)`, Angular checks if the reference changed. If you mutate the existing object (e.g., `state.items.push(newItem)`), the reference is the same, and change detection may not fire.

**Always create a new object/array** when updating state:

```typescript
// ❌ Bad (mutation)
this.state.value.items.push(item);
this.state.next(this.state.value);

// ✅ Good (immutable)
const newItems = [...this.state.value.items, item];
this.state.next({ items: newItems });
```

slides.codeSnippet:
// Update a nested property
interface User { name: string; profile: { email: string } }

updateEmail(newEmail: string) {
  const current = this.state.value.user;
  const updatedUser = {
    ...current,
    profile: { ...current.profile, email: newEmail }
  };
  this.state.next({ user: updatedUser });
}

deleteItem(id: number) {
  const currentItems = this.state.value.items;
  // Optimistically remove
  this.state.next({ items: currentItems.filter(i => i.id !== id) });

  this.http.delete(`/api/items/${id}`).subscribe({
    error: () => {
      // Rollback
      this.state.next({ items: currentItems });
      console.error('Delete failed, rolled back');
    }
  });
}
